WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Speaker 1: Welcome back to the David Pakman&nbsp;
show. I am Ferron Cousins from Ring of Fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:11.200
sitting in for David today and tomorrow. Well,&nbsp;
we also have to talk you know, we've talked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.160
lot about Republican extremists. We've talked&nbsp;
January six, Donald Trump, all of that. We got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.840
to get to some issues here, folks. And of course,&nbsp;
one of the biggest issues facing Americans today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.960
is inflation. You know, whether it's gas prices or&nbsp;
goods services, everything out there, inflation is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.520
absolutely crippling the United States economy.&nbsp;
And according to a new report from the Roosevelt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:44.000
Institute. A lot of this, if inflation, if&nbsp;
you want to call it that, is actually rooted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.360
in nothing more than corporate greed. Now, that's&nbsp;
not to say that inflation is not happening right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.360
now at a staggering and alarming rate. It&nbsp;
is, but it's happening all over the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.840
not just here in the United States. This is not&nbsp;
a U.S. problem. This is a global supply issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.880
But here in the United States, we do have&nbsp;
something a little bit unique. We have what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.600
being called now greed flirtation. And that greed&nbsp;
flirtation is corporations, of course, jacking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:23.520
up prices beyond what inflation is, forcing&nbsp;
them to pay and passing that on to consumers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.320
It's a very simple scam. And to be honest,&nbsp;
corporations, many CEOs, executives, they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.000
actually admitted to doing this in interviews&nbsp;
over the past year. They have said on the record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
that times of high inflation are the perfect&nbsp;
time for companies to raise their prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
anyway. One because they don't get blamed for it.&nbsp;
But two, inflation eventually goes away. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.720
sometimes it lasts a little longer, you know, but&nbsp;
eventually the prices will go back down. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.720
people start seeing the price tags go back down,&nbsp;
they get happy, too, like, oh, boy, it's over. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.680
then eventually the price will settle somewhere.&nbsp;
That will be more than what it was before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.880
period of inflation, but lower than what it was&nbsp;
with inflation. So consumers think they win.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.720
But that difference between the pre inflation&nbsp;
price and the post inflation price, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.120
is the corporate price gouging. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.760
this new report from the Roosevelt Institute&nbsp;
says that corporate price gouging right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:36.320
based on profits. Based on what they're paying&nbsp;
these corporations. With inflation and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:43.520
passing on to consumers is the worst level it&nbsp;
has been in the United States since the 19 1850s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:53.280
So it's been roughly 70 years. Most people alive&nbsp;
today. Have never seen corporate price gouging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:59.360
as bad as it is. And of course, this was just for&nbsp;
the year 2021 because we don't have final numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:05.360
from 2022 because we're only halfway through the&nbsp;
year. So this report was only looking at last year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.560
when inflation and price gouging was not&nbsp;
as bad as it has been so far this year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.120
If you want to know a good example, look at&nbsp;
Tyson Foods. Tyson Foods recently released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:25.040
you know, their their quarterly report. In their&nbsp;
quarterly report. They showed that inflation had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.960
caused their costs to go up. I'm just going to&nbsp;
make up numbers because I don't remember exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.760
but I do remember the percentages. So trust&nbsp;
me, let's say their costs went up 1.5 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:45.840
But the prices they raised for consumers&nbsp;
wasn't 1.5 billion. It was 2 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:55.120
So there's a $500 million gap in there&nbsp;
that is nothing more than corporate greed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.240
raising prices simply for the fact that they can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.560
And again, those weren't the exact numbers, but&nbsp;
it was the correct percentages. There was a 25%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:14.320
increase. So what we're looking at is a&nbsp;
time when the United States, more than ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.800
needs a windfall profit tax on these corporations,&nbsp;
whether it's the oil industry, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:30.160
it's the grocery stores. Manufacturers, car&nbsp;
manufacturers done matter. A windfall profits tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:38.640
could put an end to this corporate price gouging&nbsp;
that is crushing Americans every time they go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:45.200
shopping doesn't matter what they go shopping for.&nbsp;
And believe it or not, this corporate windfall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.280
profit tax is now supported by a majority&nbsp;
of American citizens. A majority of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:05:00.880
Want this windfall profits tax. It's a simple&nbsp;
solution. It's a simple solution to a growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.160
problem that affects the wallets of everybody in&nbsp;
this country, rich and poor alike. It obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.800
affects the poor a lot more than it affects&nbsp;
the rich. But everybody's paying higher prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:19.360
The windfall profits tax could put an end&nbsp;
to that. We just have to have legislators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:25.280
with the courage to actually do this. And&nbsp;
unfortunately, we do not have enough of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.760
Instead we get ideas from Biden, like coming out&nbsp;
